# NYC poll site disparate impact tests

## Executive summary

**Question.** Are higher poverty NYC census tracts less likely to have early voting poll site inside the tract, so that residents must visit a site in a different tract? ({{ r.tracts_analyzed }} tracts, {{ r.poll_sites }} poll sites.)

**Answer.** {% if s.supported %}The baseline hypothesis is supported: higher poverty tracts are more likely to have no poll site inside the tract.{% else %}The baseline hypothesis is not supported: higher poverty tracts are not more likely to have no poll site inside the tract.{% if s.opposite_and_significant %} The association runs the opposite way.{% endif %}{% endif %}


- Across the five poverty classes, {{ s.first.rate | pct }} of {{ s.first.group | lower }} tracts lack an in-tract site, against {{ s.last.rate | pct }} of {{ s.last.group | lower }} tracts. Chi-squared = {{ "%.2f" | format(c.chi2) }}, p = {{ c.p | p }}, but the effect is small (Cramér's V = {{ "%.3f" | format(c.cramers_v) }}) and the trend test points the other direction (one-sided p = {{ b.trend.p_one_sided | p }}).
- Disparate impact: tracts at {{ "%.0f" | format(HIGH_POVERTY) }}% poverty or above have an in-tract site {{ di.favorable_rate_high | pct }} of the time, tracts below that {{ di.favorable_rate_lower | pct }}. The impact ratio is {{ "%.2f" | format(di.impact_ratio) }}, which {{ "fails" if di.fails_four_fifths else "passes" }} the four-fifths rule.
- Most tracts of every kind lack an in-tract site. The overall rate is {{ s.overall_out_rate | pct }}, so a visit to another tract is the norm, not a burden that falls on poor tracts.
- Related tests that are significant after Holm correction and contradict the hypothesis: {{ s.opposite | join(", ") or "none" }}. Significant and consistent with it: {{ s.toward | join(", ") or "none" }}.
- Distance (H3 to H5, centroid to nearest site at a quarter, half, and one mile): higher poverty tracts are {{ "farther from" if s.distance_farther else "closer to" }} a poll site than lower poverty tracts.
{% set mh, ml = s.manhattan.groups %}
- By borough, Manhattan shows the largest gap: {{ mh.rate | pct }} of high poverty tracts lack an in-tract site against {{ ml.rate | pct }} of lower poverty tracts{% if s.manhattan.chi_squared %} (p = {{ s.manhattan.chi_squared.p | p }}){% endif %}; no other borough differs significantly on its own.

**Caution.** Tract containment is a proxy for the real assignment (election districts). Neighboring tracts are not independent, so p-values are probably too small. See Limits.

Tracts analyzed: {{ r.tracts_analyzed }} with a poverty rate ({{ r.tracts_with_income }} also with median income). Poll sites: {{ r.poll_sites }} ({{ s.excluded_sites }}). Tracts with a site inside: {{ r.tracts_with_site }}. Outcome: no poll site inside the tract, so voters visit a site in a different tract.

